<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1223">1223</a>–<a href="/years/1242">1242</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-europe">Mongol invasion of Europe</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> began with a raid that defeated the Rus' princes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka">Kalka</a> River in 1223 and became a full conquest under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> in 1237–1240. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a>, Vladimir and many other cities were destroyed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> fell in December 1240; the Rus' principalities then paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> for some two centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasion-of-kievan-rus.jpg" alt="Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qiushufang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1236-1242_Mongol_invasions_of_Europe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1207 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> sent his eldest son Jochi to subdue the tribes north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selenga-river">Selenga River</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a> valley, lands that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> in the Jochi Ulus. In 1222 to 1224 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jebe">Jebe</a> led a force of 30,000 through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, traditionally regarded as a reconnaissance. On 31 May 1223 they defeated a united Rus' army, together with the remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river">Battle of the Kalka River</a>, and then withdrew. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> of 1235 renewed the plan, and Batu, a son of Jochi, led the army that gathered in the Caspian steppes in the autumn of 1236.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The army first conquered Volga Bulgaria, which fell by the spring of 1237, and then defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and Alans. In November 1237 Batu sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-of-vladimir">Yuri II of Vladimir</a> demanding submission. Yuri sent gifts but avoided open conflict. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> attacked from several directions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> fell after three days, and a chronicler records that the Mongols killed its prince and princess and many of its people. They burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, besieged Vladimir on 4 February 1238 and took and burned it three days later. On 4 March the army Yuri had raised was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-sit-river">Battle of the Sit River</a>.</p><p>Batu then divided his force and ransacked fourteen cities of the north-east, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a>, Tver and Torzhok. Kozelsk resisted for seven weeks. Novgorod was spared by an early decision to surrender. In the winter of 1239 Batu sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and Pereyaslavl. His army stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in December 1240 after many days of siege and pillaged it, although the Cathedral of Saint Sophia survived. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> then moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> on Christmas Day 1241.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> is generally held to mark the end of the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, which had already been fragmenting. The principalities became part of the Jochid appanage, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> lay on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. Tribute demands continued until about 1480, though a Russian army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at Kulikovo in 1380. Colin McEvedy estimates that the population fell from 7.5 million to seven million, and many cities took over a century to recover.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> rose under Mongol rule. After the prince of Tver led an uprising in 1327, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I of Moscow</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in crushing it and received the title of Grand Prince. Most historians hold that the invasion accelerated a fragmentation already under way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
